course: technology
lesson: 25
title: Bicycles and other wheels
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A bicycle turns the push of your legs into rolling. Your feet push the pedals, the pedals turn the big chain wheel, the chain turns a small cog on the back wheel, and the back wheel pushes the bike forward.
explain: Standing still on a bike you topple at once, but when you ride you hardly have to think. While moving, you keep steering the front wheel a tiny bit towards the side you are falling to, which brings the wheels back under you; the faster you go, the easier these small corrections are.
explain: Gears change how hard each turn of the pedals is. Going up the steep hill to school, you click to a bigger back cog: you pedal more turns, but each turn is easy.
explain: A bike is one of the most energy-saving ways a person can travel, and with a helmet, lights, good brakes and a safe place to ride, it is a great way to get around.
fact: Pedals turn a chain wheel, the chain turns a cog on the back wheel, and you balance by steering and moving forward; it is easier to balance when you go faster.
fact: Moving the chain to a bigger back cog makes pedalling easier but slower, good for hills; a smaller back cog makes it harder but faster. Change gears while pedalling gently.
fact: Pulling a brake lever squeezes rubber pads onto the wheel rim or onto a disc; brakes take longer to work in the rain.
fact: Use both brakes, the front one gently: squeezing only the front brake hard can throw you over the handlebars.
fact: A helmet is a hard shell over foam that crushes in a crash and absorbs the hit; the foam only works once, so a helmet must be replaced after a crash even if it looks fine. Wear it level and strapped.
fact: A bike needs a white light at the front and a red light at the back at night; reflectors are made of tiny corner shapes that send light straight back to where it came from, such as a driver's headlights.
fact: A puncture is a hole that lets air out of the inner tube; dip the pumped-up tube in water and bubbles show where the hole is, then it can be patched or the tube replaced.
fact: An electric bike has a battery and motor that help while you pedal; in the UK the motor must stop helping at about 15.5 mph (25 km/h).
fact: E-bike batteries must be charged only with the right charger, on a hard surface, and not overnight in a bedroom or hallway.
fact: Skateboard and scooter wheels spin easily because of bearings, rings of little steel balls; ride them with a helmet and pads, away from roads.
fact: A motorbike leans into bends to turn; motorbikes are fast and hard to see, so look twice before crossing.
fact: A bike lane gives bikes their own space; lanes separated by kerbs keep cars out, but riders still watch for opening car doors and turning vehicles.
fact: A bicycle has 2 wheels, a tricycle has 3, and a car has 4.
exercise: What does the chain on a bike do? | It carries the turning of the pedals to the back wheel | It locks the bike so it can't be stolen when you leave it outside a shop | It holds the two wheels together | It makes the front wheel steer
exercise: Why is it easier to balance a bike when you go faster? | While moving, small steering turns keep the wheels under you, and faster makes it easier | A fast bike gets lighter and floats | The wind holds you up from both sides | The pedals hold you upright when they spin
exercise: Which gear should you use to ride up a steep hill? | A bigger back cog, so each pedal turn is easier | The smallest back cog, so you go faster up it | No gear, you should only pedal backwards | It doesn't matter at all, gears only change the clicking noise the chain makes
exercise: When should you change gears? | While pedalling gently | Only when the bike is standing still | Only while braking hard | While lifting the back wheel off the ground
exercise: What happens when you pull a rim brake lever? | Rubber pads squeeze the wheel rim and slow it down | The chain is pulled off the cogs | A stick pokes into the spokes and jams the wheel so it can't turn any more | The tyres let out some air
exercise: Why shouldn't you grab only the front brake hard? | It can throw you over the handlebars | It makes the back wheel spin even faster | It snaps the thin brake cable every single time | It only works when you are riding uphill
exercise: Why do brakes need extra care in the rain? | Wet brakes take longer to work | Rain makes brakes stop the bike too suddenly | Brakes switch off when wet | Rain only affects the back brake
exercise: Why must a bike helmet be replaced after a crash even if it looks fine? | Its foam only works once, crushing to absorb the hit | Its colour fades after a crash | The strap always gets longer | It becomes much too heavy to wear because the foam soaks up sweat and rain
exercise: Which lights does a bike need at night? | White at the front and red at the back | Red at the front and white at the back | Green at the front and blue at the back | Only a flashing light on the helmet
exercise: Why do reflectors seem to glow when car headlights hit them? | Tiny corner shapes send the light straight back to the driver | They have little batteries inside | They store sunlight from the day | They are painted with glow paint that heats up
exercise: How can you find a tiny hole in a bike's inner tube? | Pump it up, dip it in water and look for bubbles | Look at it in the dark with a torch | Shake it and listen for a rattle | Wait for it to go flat, then squeeze it hard all over and feel for a soft bit
exercise: When does an e-bike's motor help you, in the UK? | When you pedal, up to about 15.5 mph | All the time, just like a little motorbike | Only when you are riding downhill fast | Only when you stop pedalling and rest your legs
exercise: Where should an e-bike battery be charged? | With the right charger, on a hard surface, not overnight in a bedroom | On a soft bed so it doesn't get scratched | With any charger that fits the plug | Overnight in the hallway by the front door
exercise: What makes skateboard and scooter wheels spin so easily? | Bearings, rings of little steel balls | Oil poured on the rubber tyres every day | Tiny electric motors hidden inside each wheel | Magnets in the wheels that push them round and round
exercise: Why should you look twice for motorbikes before crossing? | They are fast and hard to see | They always drive on the pavement | They always ride on the wrong side | They are not allowed to brake
exercise: Why are some bike lanes separated by kerbs? | To keep cars out of the bikes' space | To stop bikes from going much too fast down the hill | So that bikes can park in the lane overnight | To catch rainwater for the trees along the street
exercise: Count them: 4 bikes and 1 car in the driveway. How many wheels? | 12 | 8 | 10 | 5
test: «helmet's fine, I only dropped it once and fell on it a bit, no crack» What is right? | Replace it: the foam may be crushed and only works once | Keep it, no crack means no damage | Keep it, helmets get stronger after a hit, like a muscle that has been exercised | Just tighten the strap and it's fine
test: «why do cyclists lean? motorbikes too, they're showing off» What is right? | Leaning into the bend is how a two-wheeler turns without falling over | Yes, it's only for showing off | They lean to see the road better | They lean to keep the rain off
test: It's raining and Sam rides down a hill. What is the safe way to stop? | Brake early with both brakes, the front gently | Grab only the front brake hard at the bottom | Use only his feet on the ground, dragging his shoes along | Brake hard at the last second
test: In a cartoon, a kid rides home in the dark with no lights and the cars all see him. What is wrong? | Drivers would find him hard to see: he needs white front, red back and something bright | Nothing, cars have special night eyes | Only cars need lights, not bikes | Bikes glow on their own at night
test: «e-bike is basically a motorbike, so no pedalling needed innit» What is right? | No, its motor only helps while you pedal | Yes, it goes on its own like a motorbike | Yes, but only uphill | No, e-bikes have no motor at all
test: What would happen if you changed to the smallest back cog to climb a steep hill? | Each pedal turn would be very hard | Pedalling would become very easy, like going downhill | The bike would start to roll backwards down the hill | The chain would lock the pedals in place
test: «my bike's always flat, mate says it just leaks for no reason» What is the likely cause and the fix? | A small hole in the inner tube; find it with bubbles in water and patch it | Tyres just lose air for no reason, nothing helps | The pedals push the air out, ride less | The air freezes overnight and shrinks
test: «gonna plug my e-bike in on my bed overnight, it's comfy there» What is the answer? | No, charge it on a hard surface with the right charger, not overnight in a bedroom | Fine, beds are soft and safe | Fine if you use any charger | Fine as long as the window is open
test: «scooter's fine on the main road, cars will go round me» What is right? | No, ride scooters and skateboards away from roads, with a helmet | Yes, scooters count as cars, so they're allowed to ride in the middle of the lane | Yes, if you go fast enough | Only at night is it a problem
test: You open a car door onto a bike lane. What should you do first? | Check for bikes coming before opening the door | Open it fast so bikes see it | Nothing, bikes must look out for doors, it's the cyclist's job to keep away | Ring a bell to warn cyclists
test: Count them: a family has 2 bikes, a tricycle and a car. How many wheels? | 11 | 10 | 9 | 12
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a cyclist ought not to apply the front brake alone with great force. | Such action may cause the rider to be projected over the handlebars | Such action causes the rear wheel to accelerate | Such action invariably severs the brake cable | The front brake is operative only upon an ascending gradient of some steepness
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the selection of gears upon an ascending gradient. | A larger rear sprocket renders each revolution of the pedals less arduous | The smallest rear sprocket renders each revolution of the pedals less arduous | Gears serve solely to alter the sound produced by the bicycle | The ascent should be undertaken by pedalling in reverse
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a protective helmet must be replaced following a collision, notwithstanding the absence of visible damage. | Its foam lining absorbs an impact only once, by being crushed | Its colour is impaired by any collision | Its strap is invariably lengthened by a collision | Its weight is increased by any collision so as to render it uncomfortable to wear
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of a reflector? | Minute corner-shaped surfaces return incident light directly towards its source | It contains a battery which illuminates it upon the approach of a vehicle | It stores daylight and releases it during darkness | It is coated with a substance that glows upon being heated
